## 1.2 Statement of the Problem

The ethical significance of deception has traditionally been examined within distinct disciplinary boundaries. Biomedical ethics primarily investigates deception through the doctrine of informed consent and patient autonomy. Political philosophy focuses on legitimacy, transparency, and democratic accountability. Psychology examines cognitive manipulation, coercive influence, and interpersonal abuse, while organizational ethics considers fiduciary responsibility, whistleblowing, and corporate governance. Although each discipline offers valuable insights, these perspectives often remain conceptually isolated, limiting their ability to explain deception as a broader phenomenon affecting human agency across multiple domains.

This fragmentation creates a significant theoretical gap. Similar mechanisms of deception may be judged according to entirely different ethical standards depending upon whether they occur within medicine, government, religious institutions, corporations, or private relationships. Consequently, no widely accepted interdisciplinary framework currently exists for determining when deception transitions from a morally ambiguous act into a violation of informed consent that undermines autonomous decision-making.

This limitation becomes increasingly significant in contemporary information environments. Digital communication, algorithmic decision systems, artificial intelligence, behavioral targeting, mass surveillance, and large-scale information management have expanded both the scale and sophistication of epistemic influence. Individuals routinely encounter situations in which the accuracy, completeness, or intentionality of information cannot be independently verified. Existing ethical models frequently evaluate these circumstances within isolated institutional contexts rather than recognizing their shared dependence upon truthful access to materially relevant information.

Moreover, discussions surrounding transparency often become polarized between two competing positions. One perspective argues that virtually all deception is ethically impermissible because truthfulness represents an unconditional moral obligation. The opposing perspective maintains that deception may be justified whenever authorities claim legitimate institutional interests such as security, organizational stability, competitive advantage, or social harmony. Neither position adequately accounts for the complexity of situations in which partial disclosure, proportional secrecy, and competing duties coexist.

This dissertation argues that the ethical evaluation of deception should instead focus upon its consequences for informed agency. Specifically, deception becomes ethically significant when it deprives individuals of information necessary to understand relevant risks, evaluate available alternatives, or voluntarily withdraw from relationships, institutions, or systems. The absence of such information compromises meaningful consent regardless of the particular institutional setting in which deception occurs.

Accordingly, the central problem addressed by this dissertation is the absence of an integrated ethical framework capable of distinguishing ethically permissible information management from deception that materially impairs autonomous decision-making. By proposing the concepts of the Consent Threshold, Epistemic Self-Defense, and the Witness Doctrine, this research seeks to provide a coherent normative model applicable across interpersonal, governmental, institutional, and technological contexts.

Rather than treating deception solely as an issue of honesty, legality, or institutional policy, this study reframes it as a question of agency. The guiding assumption is that autonomy depends not upon complete knowledge but upon sufficient access to materially relevant information necessary for informed decision-making. Ethical legitimacy therefore depends upon preserving the informational conditions under which genuine consent remains possible.

